Window bounds published to AccessKit (Task 4 of docs/plans/20261031-e2e-ui-and-coverage.md). AccessKit places each
node in the bounds its adapter was last told for the window, and GPUI's Linux backends define
`a11y_update_window_bounds` but never call it, so `accesskit_unix` gave AT-SPI clients window-local extents: a screen
reader's or a test driver's `Component.get_extents(SCREEN)` landed at the screen's origin, and nothing could point at a
control (the conformance suite's `atspi` driver clicks and drags by those extents; `accesskit_windows` maps to the
screen itself through `ClientToScreen`, so Windows needs nothing). Both backends now report the bounds when the
adapter starts and on every geometry change. X11 translates the client window's origin to root coordinates
(`ConfigureNotify` can be parent-relative or stale during a resize), refreshes on `ReparentNotify` even without a
configure that follows it, rounds the device-pixel size it rebuilds, and takes the whole client surface as the
nodes' origin (the client-drawn shadow insets are already in GPUI's node coordinates). Wayland gives a client no
global position, so it reports the scaled surface at a zero origin, a scale change included. The other platforms are
unchanged. Checked on a private Xvfb and a11y bus by `conformance/selftests/test_linux_ui_integration.py` (the app,
and the `a11y_inset_probe` example's window with client-drawn insets) and by the suite's ui layer.
scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to the verified crates.io archive.

